<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaziantep</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aintab, Antep</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaziantep, historically Aintab or Antep, is a city in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> near the Syrian border. Occupied by British and then French troops after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, it resisted a long French siege in 1920–1921 before surrendering in February 1921, and the Turkish parliament added the title Gazi, 'veteran', to its name in recognition. It is today a major industrial city, known for pistachios and its cuisine.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaziantep.jpg" alt="Gaziantep" width="320" height="137" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Klaus-Peter Simon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AntepKale2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
